// Direct callback observations from pinned PyBot 7d63c7f.
const REPLIES: &[&str] = &[
    "Roast potatoes in the same oven for the last 45 minutes (boil them 10 minutes first, drain and shake them in hot oil and salt), and peas or greens with butter. Gravy from the tin is fine. If there is room, a tray of carrots, parsnips and onion can go in beside the bird, so the whole dinner comes out of one oven.",
    "Rice, and naan or a chapati if you have them: a mug of rice to one and a half of water, 12 minutes with the lid on, and a spoon of yoghurt or raita beside it.",
    "Rosemary, garlic and black pepper are the classic on lamb: push slivers of garlic and rosemary into a leg, or rub chops with oil, salt, crushed garlic and a pinch of cumin. Anchovy mashed into the rub, or a spoon of mint sauce stirred through yoghurt, is the other one that works. Salt the meat the night before if you have the time.",
    "Tomatoes want salt, a little sugar if they are pale, and a herb that is not shy: basil torn in at the end for a salad, oregano or thyme in the pan for a sauce, and garlic cooked gently rather than browned. Salt them ten minutes before they go in and the juices come out and turn into the sauce.",
    "Lemon, garlic, thyme and paprika: rub the skin with oil, salt, lemon zest, crushed garlic and a spoon of paprika, and put half a lemon inside. For pieces, the same mix with cumin instead of paprika is what most traybakes want.",
    "Beef wants salt and coarse black pepper, and not much else: salt a steak 40 minutes before it goes in the pan, or rub a roast with mustard, thyme and horseradish before it goes in the oven. A spoon of horseradish in creme fraiche is the sauce, not the seasoning.",
    "Season the meat before it is shaped, not after: salt, black pepper and a little onion or mustard in the mix, then fry a spoonful to taste before you commit the lot. A burger needs nothing on the outside but salt, pepper and a hot pan.",
    "Tofu has no salt or fat of its own, so it needs both: soy sauce, sesame oil, garlic and ginger, or a marinade it sits in for half an hour after being pressed. Fry it hard in a little oil first, until the edges are brown, and sauce it after - added wet, it steams and stays pale.",
    "Ribs want sugar in the rub and acid in the sauce: brown sugar, paprika, salt, black pepper, garlic powder and a little cumin, rubbed on the night before or an hour ahead. Cook them low, covered, for two to three hours at 150C, then paint with barbecue sauce and finish them hot for ten minutes. The sugar is what makes the crust, and it burns above 200C, so sauce goes on late.",
    "New potatoes and peas, or rice with greens wilted in a pan with butter. A squeeze of lemon and yoghurt with chopped dill is all the sauce it needs, and both take less time than the fish.",
    "Bread and butter, or a cheese toastie cut in fingers for dipping. Something crunchy on top - toasted seeds, croutons, a handful of crisps - turns a bowl of soup into a meal.",
    "Plain and starchy wants something wet on top: a sauce, a stew, a curry, or the juices from the pan, plus one sharp thing because the starch has no acid of its own. So pasta wants a sauce with garlic and a little of the water it cooked in; rice wants a curry or a daal and a squeeze of lemon; bread wants olive oil, tomato or soup.",
    "Rich food wants something sharp and something crisp beside it: pickle or chutney, a few grapes or a sliced pear, crackers or warm bread, and a handful of leaves with a mustardy dressing if it has to be a meal. The rule underneath is that fat needs acid - the same reason chips want vinegar and pork wants apple sauce.",
    "Something starchy, something green, and something sharp or juicy: potatoes or rice or bread, a green vegetable with butter or oil on it, and a squeeze of lemon, a spoon of yoghurt, or a tomato salad. Keep it to one of each and it works with nearly anything - the sharp thing is what stops the plate tasting flat.",
    "Fish fingers, a fried egg or a burger, and peas, pickles or coleslaw: something sharp cuts the fat.",
    "Butter, salt and pepper, and then the wet, sharp things next to it: coleslaw, a tomato and red onion salad with lime, or a spoon of chilli butter melted over the cobs. It sits beside a barbecue, a roast chicken or a bowl of chilli without any more cooking, and 2 minutes under a hot grill after boiling blisters the kernels.",
    "Mash and onion gravy: boil the potatoes 15 minutes, mash them with butter and milk, and soften sliced onion in the sausage pan with a spoon of flour and a splash of stock for the gravy. Peas take 3 minutes, and the pan the sausages cooked in is the flavour.",
    "Mash, or dumplings dropped in for the last 25 minutes with the lid on.",
    "Fish takes lemon, dill and butter, and salt before it cooks rather than after: zest the lemon over it, lay dill on top and put a knob of butter on each piece. Paprika or fennel seed for a fillet that will be fried hard.",
    "Garlic bread and a green salad with a sharp dressing, because the salad is what cuts through the sauce: tear the bread, rub it with garlic, oil and salt and give it 8 minutes in a hot oven while the pasta cooks.",
    "Buttercream is 100g soft butter to 200g icing sugar, beaten with a splash of milk.",
    "Two of these three, and the plate balances itself: something starchy (new potatoes, rice, bread), a vegetable cooked hot and quick (greens with butter, roasted carrots, a sharp salad), and something cool or sharp to cut through it (yoghurt, pickles, a squeeze of lemon). So a roast takes potatoes and greens, anything in a sauce takes rice or bread and a salad, and a barbecue takes coleslaw and pickles.",
    "Rice, or tortillas and a bowl of grated cheese and sour cream on the table.",
    "Peas or greens with butter, and bread and butter to mop the plate. A spoon of mustard or horseradish on the side cuts through a rich pie, and both take 5 minutes from frozen.",
    "New potatoes with butter and mint, peas, and yoghurt stirred with chopped mint and lemon instead of mint sauce. Roast carrots and parsnips if the oven is on anyway; both take about 40 minutes beside the meat.",
    "New potatoes, peas, and mustard or pickles: a spoon of mustard in the juices in the pan makes the sauce, and cabbage braised in butter fills the plate for very little.",
    "Garlic bread and a green salad, and keep a mug of the pasta water back to loosen the sauce with.",
    "Pick four dinners you already cook and plan the week on one list before you shop, then cook once for two nights: a big chilli or a tray of roast vegetables tonight, the rest in a pasta bake or wraps tomorrow, and one night that needs no cooking at all. Put one double batch in the freezer for the night you are late home, and keep two fallbacks in the cupboard - pasta and a tin of tomatoes, rice and eggs - for the night the plan falls apart.",
    "Cheap weeks come from the same eight things: rice, pasta, tins of tomatoes, chickpeas or lentils, onions, carrots, frozen veg and eggs. Pick four dinners you already cook from those and put them on the fridge: a big chilli or bolognese stretched with lentils so it eats twice, a tray of roast vegetables with the rest in a pasta bake, a lentil dhal, and beans on toast. That is about 20 pounds a head for the week, and nothing in it goes off.",
    "Cook two big pots on one afternoon and the week is half done: a chilli or bolognese and a curry or dhal, each cooled and split into portions, then frozen flat in bags so they thaw in minutes. Cook rice and pasta fresh while a portion reheats, label the bags with the date, and eat them within three months.",
    "Ten-minute dinners: pasta with garlic, chilli and olive oil; eggs on toast; or cold rice fried with frozen veg, an egg and soy sauce. Tell me what is in the fridge and I will pick one.",
    "Twenty minutes is enough for: a stir-fry, with the rice on before anything else; pasta with garlic, chilli and a tin of tomatoes; or an omelette with bread and salad, which is the quickest of the three.",
    "Twenty-five minutes is enough for: a stir-fry, with the rice on before anything else; pasta with garlic, chilli and a tin of tomatoes; an omelette with bread and salad; or vegetables cut small and roasted 25 minutes at 200C while couscous steeps in the same time.",
    "Half an hour is enough for: chicken pieces or sausages in the oven while the potatoes boil, a tray of vegetables cut small roasted with couscous, or pasta baked with a tin of tomatoes and cheese. Put the oven on first and the kettle second, and with an hour you can leave a chilli or a curry to simmer.",
];
